    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe new directory plugin is replacing netrw as a local file browser, but it's missing the ability to differentiate symbolic links from files and directories, and executable files from regular files.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nMy preference would be to add classifing characters to the end of more filetypes, similar to what `ls -F` does and what netrw did.  This way entries could be highlighted based on the classifier.  `dir.lua` already uses this approach for directories by using a trailing `/` as a classifier.\n\nUsers that don't want to see the classifier could simply conceal them without losing syntax highlighting.\n\nAn alternative approach could be to use extmarks for syntax highlighting, although I'm less familiar with how those work.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> add classifing characters to the end of more filetypes, similar to what `ls -F` does and what netrw did\n\nThe buffer contents are intentionally designed to only have the filename, so that yanking/copying the names can be used for `:!`, commands, etc. However, adding virtual text or other decorations could be worth exploring. \n\nBut meanwhile, you can use the `DirReadPost` event to modify dir listings as you want: https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/41138",
